In the time before maps had names, there was a small lake that slept beneath a hill of reeds. Its surface wore a velvet cloak of moonlight each night, and fishers whispered that the moon came to drink there, leaving a trail of silver scales on the water.

One evening, a stubborn farmer named Leto, who counted coins the way others counted breaths, tripped on a root and tumbled into the lake. But the lake did not drown him; it spoke. "If you listen," it murmured, "I will tell you where a seed of old wealth lies, guarded by the patience of the watercat." Leto wanted wealth more than breath, so he listened.

From the whispers, a single green seed rose from the depth, shaped like a teardrop. The lake told him to plant it at dawn where the reeds grew thickest, and to wait. Leto did as told, though doubt gnawed at his heels like reeds in the wind. Days turned to weeks; the seed sprouted into a tree that bore fruit only at midnight.

When the fruit finally ripened, it opened to reveal not gold, but a mirror of the farmer’s own eyes. The lake spoke once more: "Wealth is not the harvest, but the seeing. Those who truly see riches in others discover they already own the harvest within." And so Leto, rich in empathy, watched the moon drink the lake forevermore.
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

